 This framework targets the second failure mode. Under latent frailty heterogeneity, high-risk individuals die earlier, so the surviving risk set becomes progressively "healthier." With a constant individual baseline hazard this selection produces a concave cohort cumulative hazard, even under a null treatment effect. With an increasing baseline, the sign of the curvature also depends on baseline growth. When selection concentrates frailty heterogeneity differently across cohorts, the resulting curvature differences produce strong non-proportional hazards and can drive misleading contrasts for estimands that condition on the evolving risk set.
 
-KCOR does not assert a causal interpretation of the resulting contrast; rather, it highlights residual differences that cannot be attributed to frailty-driven selection under the working model, flagging regimes that demand substantive explanation beyond selection artifacts. KCOR does not identify constant multiplicative hazard effects as separate parameters; they are not separately identifiable from baseline scale. Under minimal aggregated data, $\theta_{0,d}$ is estimated from the quiet-window hazard trajectory under the specified Gompertz model (§2.4.4). Non-multiplicative effects (additive or time-varying) may distort curvature and reduce identifiability. Interpretation is therefore conditional on the adequacy of the gamma-frailty working model, the Gompertz structure, and the associated diagnostics.
